# Experiment 10

## Phishing awareness simulation using Gophish

## Aim

To configure an authorised training campaign, connect its sending profile, email template, landing page and test group, and interpret the recorded events to improve phishing awareness.

## Theory

Phishing uses a misleading message to induce an unsafe action, such as following a deceptive link or disclosing information. Common cues include a look-alike sender domain, urgency, unexpected attachments and a mismatch between link text and destination. Spear phishing targets a particular person or group, while smishing and vishing use text messages and voice calls.

Gophish organises a simulation around five components. A sending profile supplies SMTP settings. An email template contains the training message and placeholders. A landing page receives the click. A user group defines the consenting test audience. A campaign combines these items with a destination URL and schedule.

Tracking events need careful interpretation. An open event generally depends on an image request and may be suppressed by image blocking or generated by a proxy. A security scanner may follow a link. A recorded click is therefore a measurement requiring context, rather than conclusive proof of a person's action. SMTP acceptance also does not guarantee inbox placement.

## Requirements

Gophish on the chosen lab computer, a browser, a controlled SMTP server or mail catcher, and consenting test recipients. Use an awareness landing page with credential collection disabled. The PDF's `lab.local` values represent the course's sample environment and require a configured mail setup.

